WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.670
It's not often that you see a gravestone with
a correction on it.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.580
But then, it's not often that you find a story
as strange as this one.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.230
I'm just outside Huelva, in southern Spain,

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:14.749
and a little distance away is the grave of
the Man Who Never Was.

00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:17.320
In 1943, as the Second World War raged on,

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.730
the body of Major William Martin, of the British
Royal Marines,

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.020
was discovered floating off the coast here.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.580
Chained to him was a briefcase, and in it:
a letter with details about

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.720
the Allied armies' plans to attack Greece
across the Mediterranean Sea.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.090
It wasn't 'here are all our secret war plans',

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.860
but it was close; it was a friendly letter
between generals

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.880
that gave away all the necessary details.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.400
British Intelligence raced to recover the
briefcase

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.130
before German spies could look through it,

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.290
but they were too late.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.100
Yes, Britain got its documents back:

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.970
but not before a copy of them was on its way
to Hitler,

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:54.120
who then moved huge numbers of troops, tanks
and boats to Greece,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.620
ready to repel the British attack.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.740
Hitler was probably quite surprised, then,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.800
when the Allies attacked Sicily,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.920
off the southern coast of Italy, and not Greece.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.970
And won, with a fraction of the losses that
they could have suffered.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.750
There never was any Major William Martin.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:11.750
He was fictional.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.690
But there was a man called Glyndwr Michael.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.750
He was a Welshman in London in World War 2,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.290
homeless, penniless, friendless.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.400
He died on the streets after eating poison;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.940
perhaps deliberately, perhaps by desperately
eating food that was left out for rats.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.230
We'll never know.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.380
According to official records,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.580
his body was "removed out of England", and
well, that's true:

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.659
his body was held in a mortuary for a while,

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:38.830
then dressed in the correct uniform and given
painstakingly forged personal documents:

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.390
a receipt for a wedding ring, a photo and
love letter from a fiancée,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.950
a bus ticket, some keys, some other paperwork.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.890
Then the body was put onboard a submarine
which sailed to the coast of Spain.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.590
And quietly, on a dark night in April 1943,

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.450
the boat's crew let the body drift with the
currents.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.619
The plan was called, rather gruesomely, Operation
Mincemeat.

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:02.590
British Intelligence had chosen their target
well.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:03.840
Spain had plenty of German spies,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.283
and the Spanish head of intelligence was the
sort of man

00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:08.000
who would claim the discovery as his own,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.159
and then travel to Berlin to deliver copies
personally,

00:02:11.159 --> 00:02:14.689
and perhaps even embellish the story of how
it was found

00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:18.780
to make himself look better and the tip a
bit seem more credible.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:19.780
Which he did.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.760
And of course, all the desperate British efforts
to recover the documents were a sham.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.290
And by 1943, the team at Bletchley Park had
cracked the Enigma cypher

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.709
and were listening to German communications:

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:34.439
less than a month after Glyndwr's body had
been released,

00:02:34.439 --> 00:02:39.549
the codebreakers intercepted a signal that
showed that the scheme had worked perfectly.

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:43.109
Britain took Sicily, used it as a beachhead
to get to Italy,

00:02:43.109 --> 00:02:45.689
and German war plans were in tatters.

00:02:45.689 --> 00:02:51.269
Major Martin, by then, had been buried with
full military honours and an appropriate gravestone.

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:52.560
The truth was still top secret.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.779
It wasn't until decades later that his real
identity was revealed:

00:02:56.779 --> 00:02:59.799
and so the grave was corrected.

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:03.109
Glyndwr Michael, served as Major William Martin.

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:03.779
Rest in peace.

